  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Tennessee has received $11.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government has issued 21,743 awards in the state, with an average award size of $538K. Obligations are up 6.8% year over year. The Department of Energy is the largest buyer in Tennessee, obligating $7.5B so far in FY2026. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $6.2B. Other sizable buyers and sectors include the Department of Defense at $1.7B, Remediation Services at $772M, and Engineering Services at $769M. Top contractors include CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC at $3.3B and UT-BATTELLE LLC at $2.8B. These totals are contract obligations and reflect fiscal year to date activity; FY2026 totals are partial and cover the current fiscal year period only.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Tennessee changed year to year?",
        "body": "Obligations are up 6.8% year over year. Tennessee recorded $10.8B in FY21, $11.4B in FY22, $11.2B in FY23, $10.8B in FY24, $11B in FY25, and $11.7B in FY26 to date. The pattern shows multiple years near the $11B level with FY26 to date at $11.7B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate federal spending in Tennessee?",
        "body": "The Department of Energy is the dominant buyer with $7.5B in obligations. The Department of Defense is the second largest buyer at $1.7B. CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC and UT-BATTELLE LLC are the top vendors at $3.3B and $2.8B respectively, and Facilities Support Services is the largest industry at $6.2B."
      }
    ]
  },
